# Serving Static HTML Content

When the goal is to serve HTML content that already exists as a complete document, keep it as an `.html` file (`fileType: "file"`) and serve it from a small `http`-type handler. Avoid embedding the HTML in a JS/TS template string (it loses syntax highlighting, linting, and independent editability).

## Choosing how to serve it

Two patterns exist:

1. Deno text import
2. `serveFile`, which fetches the HTML file from esm.town

**Default for a fixed, known file: a text import (`with { type: "text" }`).**
Resolves the file content once, at module load / publish time — not on every
request — so there's no per-request network hop at all, and the handler stays
a plain sync function:

```ts
import html from "./index.html" with { type: "text" };

export default function (_req: Request) {
  return new Response(html, { headers: { "Content-Type": "text/html" } });
}
```

Use this whenever the file being served is a relative, author-time-known
path — the overwhelmingly common case for "serve this one HTML file."

Deno also supports dynamic text imports:

```ts
const html = (await import("./index.html", { with: { type: "text" } })).default;
```

**`serveFile` from `std/utils`.** Reads the file via esm.town fetch API on
every request, then sets `Content-Type` automatically by extension:

```ts
import { serveFile } from "https://esm.town/v/std/utils/index.ts";

export default async function (req: Request) {
  return serveFile("/index.html");
}
```

Reach for this when the path is dynamic — resolved from the incoming
request (a wildcard route serving a whole directory of assets) — since a
static import can't express that. Not a good default for a single known
file: it pays a network round-trip a static import avoids, in exchange for
automatic content-type detection you don't need when you're serving one
fixed file with a known type.
